1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,782
Where is the Rigveda's most sacred river today?

2
00:00:02,782 --> 00:00:06,259
Look from above and the desert shows only sand.

3
00:00:06,259 --> 00:00:08,345
But in ISRO's satellite images,

4
00:00:08,345 --> 00:00:10,431
lines show up beneath the sand.

5
00:00:10,431 --> 00:00:14,256
These lines look like the path of an old river. Come,

6
00:00:14,256 --> 00:00:16,690
let's unfold this story with proof.

7
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:18,635
Imagine you are up in the sky,

8
00:00:18,635 --> 00:00:22,525
looking from Haryana towards Rajasthan and Gujarat.

9
00:00:22,525 --> 00:00:25,248
Below there are fields, villages, and desert.

10
00:00:25,248 --> 00:00:28,360
But the satellite's eye catches something else.

11
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:32,639
Moisture buried under the ground, and thin strips of old sand,

12
00:00:32,639 --> 00:00:36,140
as if someone had drawn a river with a pencil.

13
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:40,338
Very ancient texts say a great river once flowed here.

14
00:00:40,338 --> 00:00:44,536
Its name was Saraswati. The Rigveda calls it sacred and powerful.

15
00:00:44,536 --> 00:00:48,734
And the Mahabharata says that river vanished into the desert.

16
00:00:48,734 --> 00:00:52,550
So is the line seen by the satellite that lost river?

17
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:56,161
The answer is not that simple. Scientists are divided,

18
00:00:56,161 --> 00:00:59,771
and so are historians. Some say yes, this is the Saraswati.

19
00:00:59,771 --> 00:01:02,660
Some say the name came from somewhere else.

20
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:05,548
In this video we will look at the texts, satellite, excavations and debate,

21
00:01:05,548 --> 00:01:09,520
one by one. Every claim will come with its label.

22
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,392
First, let's go to the texts,

23
00:01:11,392 --> 00:01:14,013
because the story starts from there.

24
00:01:14,013 --> 00:01:17,758
The Rigveda is counted among the oldest texts in the world.

25
00:01:17,758 --> 00:01:20,753
It has many mantras in praise of rivers.

26
00:01:20,753 --> 00:01:22,626
And it has one hymn

27
00:01:22,626 --> 00:01:26,370
that lists the rivers in a line, like a map.

28
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:30,644
This hymn is called the Nadistuti Sukta, meaning praise of rivers,

29
00:01:30,644 --> 00:01:32,198
a song in their praise.

30
00:01:32,198 --> 00:01:36,084
In it, the rivers are listed from east to west.

31
00:01:36,084 --> 00:01:39,192
First the Ganga, then the Yamuna, then the Saraswati, then the Shutudri,

32
00:01:39,192 --> 00:01:42,300
which we today call the Sutlej. That means the Saraswati

33
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:45,020
was somewhere between the Yamuna and the Sutlej.

34
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:48,758
This is not a small thing. The text is not just naming it,

35
00:01:48,758 --> 00:01:52,156
it is also giving the location. Look at today's map,

36
00:01:52,156 --> 00:01:55,894
and between the Yamuna and the Sutlej lies the region of Haryana.

37
00:01:55,894 --> 00:01:57,253
And that is the very region

38
00:01:57,253 --> 00:02:00,311
where the satellite sees lines of an old river.

39
00:02:00,311 --> 00:02:02,350
This is where the story gets interesting.

40
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:07,262
Another mantra of the Rigveda describes the Saraswati as a very big river.

41
00:02:07,262 --> 00:02:10,537
He says the Saraswati comes out of the mountains,

42
00:02:10,537 --> 00:02:12,994
and goes straight to the sea.

43
00:02:12,994 --> 00:02:17,906
On the way, she feeds living beings with her milk-like water.

44
00:02:17,906 --> 00:02:22,000
So this was no rain-fed drain, it was a full flowing river.

45
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,444
Now let's come to the Mahabharata,

46
00:02:24,444 --> 00:02:27,865
which is considered a later text.

47
00:02:27,865 --> 00:02:32,752
In its Shalya Parva, the story of the Saraswati changes.

48
00:02:32,752 --> 00:02:36,662
Here the river reaches the desert and dries up.

49
00:02:36,662 --> 00:02:42,038
She sinks into the ground. The place where she vanished,

50
00:02:42,038 --> 00:02:44,970
that holy site is called Vinasana.

51
00:02:44,970 --> 00:02:48,507
Vinasana itself means the place where something is destroyed,

52
00:02:48,507 --> 00:02:49,568
where something disappears.

53
00:02:49,568 --> 00:02:53,458
The text even gives the reason for this, like a story.

54
00:02:53,458 --> 00:02:57,349
So think about it: in the Rigveda, the Saraswati flows from the mountains to the sea.

55
00:02:57,349 --> 00:03:00,885
By the time of the Mahabharata, she is lost in the desert.

56
00:03:00,885 --> 00:03:02,300
What happened in between?

57
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:05,842
The text raised the question, now it's science's turn.

58
00:03:05,842 --> 00:03:08,499
If there really was a river here,

59
00:03:08,499 --> 00:03:13,369
then its marks must be left in the ground. When a river dries up,

60
00:03:13,369 --> 00:03:16,911
it leaves its path behind. Sand, gravel,

61
00:03:16,911 --> 00:03:18,682
and moisture buried below.

62
00:03:18,682 --> 00:03:23,110
To find these, scientists took help from the sky.

63
00:03:23,110 --> 00:03:26,677
This method is called remote sensing, meaning from far away,

64
00:03:26,677 --> 00:03:29,175
reading the land through the eye of a satellite.

65
00:03:29,175 --> 00:03:31,672
A satellite doesn't just take photos. It also catches moisture,

66
00:03:31,672 --> 00:03:34,526
soil color and heat.

67
00:03:34,526 --> 00:03:36,309
The old path of a dried-up river

68
00:03:36,309 --> 00:03:39,520
looks a little different from the land around it.

69
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:44,252
Scientists at ISRO's Regional Remote Sensing Service Centre did exactly this.

70
00:03:44,252 --> 00:03:48,985
They took data from the Wide Field Sensor of the Indian Remote Sensing satellite.

71
00:03:48,985 --> 00:03:50,562
And in north-west India,

72
00:03:50,562 --> 00:03:53,717
they made a map of palaeochannels buried under the ground.

73
00:03:53,717 --> 00:03:56,478
A palaeochannel means the path of an old river,

74
00:03:56,478 --> 00:03:58,450
which is now buried.

75
00:03:58,450 --> 00:04:01,316
The resolution of this sensor was 188 metres.

76
00:04:01,316 --> 00:04:04,181
Resolution means how big the smallest piece of the picture

77
00:04:04,181 --> 00:04:06,228
is on the ground.

78
00:04:06,228 --> 00:04:09,912
That means anything smaller than 188 metres can't be seen separately.

79
00:04:09,912 --> 00:04:14,416
But to see the long paths of a river, this was enough.

80
00:04:14,416 --> 00:04:18,100
And in the images, long, wide lines stood out clearly.

81
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:20,729
But just looking from the sky is not enough.

82
00:04:20,729 --> 00:04:23,357
You also have to go to the ground and check it.

83
00:04:23,357 --> 00:04:27,864
Even today in Haryana, a small stream called Sarsuti flows.

84
00:04:27,864 --> 00:04:28,990
It is seasonal,

85
00:04:28,990 --> 00:04:33,121
meaning it carries water only in the rainy season. Just listen to the name,

86
00:04:33,121 --> 00:04:35,750
Sarsuti, how similar it is to Saraswati.

87
00:04:35,750 --> 00:04:37,026
In 2009,

88
00:04:37,026 --> 00:04:42,980
a study by ISRO scientists was published in the Journal of the Geological Society of India.

89
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:47,233
It used satellite images and ground checks, both together.

90
00:04:47,233 --> 00:04:50,635
The result was that today's Sarsuti stream

91
00:04:50,635 --> 00:04:54,463
connects to that large buried river network.

92
00:04:54,463 --> 00:04:57,440
That same network they called the Vedic Saraswati.

93
00:04:57,440 --> 00:04:59,601
Then came the Central Ground Water Board,

94
00:04:59,601 --> 00:05:02,843
which keeps track of the country's underground water.

95
00:05:02,843 --> 00:05:05,004
They drilled along these paths,

96
00:05:05,004 --> 00:05:07,525
meaning they made deep holes in the ground to check.

97
00:05:07,525 --> 00:05:11,488
Deep below, they found reserves of fresh water. These are called aquifers,

98
00:05:11,488 --> 00:05:15,810
meaning layers of sand and rock inside the earth that are full of water.

99
00:05:15,810 --> 00:05:19,986
And the most interesting thing. Along the banks of this old river,

100
00:05:19,986 --> 00:05:22,909
the ruins of many Harappan settlements are found.

101
00:05:22,909 --> 00:05:27,503
Harappan means the Indus Valley Civilization, India's oldest city civilization.

102
00:05:27,503 --> 00:05:30,844
People always settle near a river.

103
00:05:30,844 --> 00:05:35,020
So these ruins tell us there was once plenty of water here.

104
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:39,345
So this is the picture so far. The scriptures say,

105
00:05:39,345 --> 00:05:42,096
the Saraswati was between the Yamuna and the Sutlej.

106
00:05:42,096 --> 00:05:45,242
The satellite shows the path of the buried river there.

107
00:05:45,242 --> 00:05:47,600
Drilling shows fresh water underneath.

108
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:51,925
And old settlements are found on the banks. It seems the story is over.

109
00:05:51,925 --> 00:05:55,070
But the real debate starts right here.

110
00:05:55,070 --> 00:05:59,212
In science, a single piece of evidence is not enough. That there was an old river here,

111
00:05:59,212 --> 00:06:01,095
most people accept this.

112
00:06:01,095 --> 00:06:04,861
But was this the same Saraswati of the Rigveda? And if it was,

113
00:06:04,861 --> 00:06:09,757
was it really a great river flowing from the mountains to the sea?

114
00:06:09,757 --> 00:06:11,640
Here the experts split.

115
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:15,264
The first debate is about the source of the water. Some scientists say,

116
00:06:15,264 --> 00:06:18,558
this river received water from Himalayan glaciers.

117
00:06:18,558 --> 00:06:21,193
A glacier means a river of ice frozen on the mountains,

118
00:06:21,193 --> 00:06:23,829
which melts slowly. Others say,

119
00:06:23,829 --> 00:06:26,135
in the Bronze Age,

120
00:06:26,135 --> 00:06:28,770
it was filled only by monsoon rain.

121
00:06:28,770 --> 00:06:32,192
Understand the difference. A river fed by glaciers keeps flowing even in summer,

122
00:06:32,192 --> 00:06:35,957
it doesn't dry up easily. A rain-fed river fills up in the monsoon,

123
00:06:35,957 --> 00:06:38,695
and becomes thin in the other seasons.

124
00:06:38,695 --> 00:06:40,064
So the question was,

125
00:06:40,064 --> 00:06:43,487
what kind of river did the Harappan people live beside?

126
00:06:43,487 --> 00:06:45,540
One piece of research gave the answer.

127
00:06:45,540 --> 00:06:49,392
This research was published in a scientific journal called Nature Communications.

128
00:06:49,392 --> 00:06:51,703
It used geomorphology,

129
00:06:51,703 --> 00:06:54,785
that is, reading the shape of the land to understand its history.

130
00:06:54,785 --> 00:06:58,637
Dating was also done, that is, how old the sand is,

131
00:06:58,637 --> 00:07:00,178
worked out through science.

132
00:07:00,178 --> 00:07:04,030
And they found out when the Sutlej moved away from this route.

133
00:07:04,030 --> 00:07:09,310
The result was surprising. Until about 8,000 years ago, the Sutlej

134
00:07:09,310 --> 00:07:13,007
had already moved away from the Ghaggar's course.

135
00:07:13,007 --> 00:07:18,815
That is, long before the Harappan cities were settled. Meaning when the cities were built,

136
00:07:18,815 --> 00:07:23,568
the big Sutlej was not flowing along this route.

137
00:07:23,568 --> 00:07:28,320
The cities were settled on its old, abandoned course.

138
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:30,873
The second debate is about the name. The Ghaggar-Hakra,

139
00:07:30,873 --> 00:07:35,554
that is, this dry river running from Haryana towards Rajasthan.

140
00:07:35,554 --> 00:07:38,532
Is this what the Rigveda called Saraswati?

141
00:07:38,532 --> 00:07:42,362
Historian Romila Thapar says this identification is disputed,

142
00:07:42,362 --> 00:07:44,915
that is, it can't be taken as certain.

143
00:07:44,915 --> 00:07:49,170
According to her, many questions on this are still unanswered.

144
00:07:49,170 --> 00:07:52,716
Scientist and writer Rajesh Kochhar says something different.

145
00:07:52,716 --> 00:07:55,868
In his book The Vedic People, he argues

146
00:07:55,868 --> 00:07:59,414
that the early Saraswati may have been the Helmand river of Afghanistan.

147
00:07:59,414 --> 00:08:03,748
The old name of that region was Haraxvati. Haraxvati and Saraswati,

148
00:08:03,748 --> 00:08:06,900
the two words sound quite similar.

149
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:10,601
In this thinking, there is a word, toponym,

150
00:08:10,601 --> 00:08:13,839
that is, the name of a place or a river.

151
00:08:13,839 --> 00:08:18,466
According to Kochhar's argument, when people moved ahead,

152
00:08:18,466 --> 00:08:21,242
they carried the names along with them.

153
00:08:21,242 --> 00:08:27,256
Just like people today give a new city the name of an old neighbourhood.

154
00:08:27,256 --> 00:08:30,494
On the other side, scientists of the ISRO study

155
00:08:30,494 --> 00:08:33,270
link the Ghaggar-Hakra itself with the Saraswati.

156
00:08:33,270 --> 00:08:35,938
So what is known, and what is not?

157
00:08:35,938 --> 00:08:39,367
It is known that there are old river routes here.

158
00:08:39,367 --> 00:08:42,035
It is known that there is fresh water underneath.

159
00:08:42,035 --> 00:08:45,084
But exactly when and why this river dried up,

160
00:08:45,084 --> 00:08:48,132
nobody has a firm timeline for that yet.

161
00:08:48,132 --> 00:08:50,800
Some big questions are still open.

162
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:51,886
Let's start with tectonics.

163
00:08:51,886 --> 00:08:55,866
Tectonics means the slow shifting of the plates under the ground.

164
00:08:55,866 --> 00:08:58,399
When the land rises and sinks a little,

165
00:08:58,399 --> 00:09:00,933
rivers change their course.

166
00:09:00,933 --> 00:09:05,999
Did some such movement turn this river's water somewhere else?

167
00:09:05,999 --> 00:09:08,170
The exact time is still not certain.

168
00:09:08,170 --> 00:09:11,176
Then comes the question of rivers changing direction.

169
00:09:11,176 --> 00:09:15,185
Research has come out on the Sutlej moving away.

170
00:09:15,185 --> 00:09:19,193
But when and how the Yamuna's course changed,

171
00:09:19,193 --> 00:09:22,700
that is still not fully clear. Just think,

172
00:09:22,700 --> 00:09:28,212
if the Sutlej and the Yamuna both once fed water this way,

173
00:09:28,212 --> 00:09:32,220
then this must really have been a big river.

174
00:09:32,220 --> 00:09:34,626
And finally, the question of climate.

175
00:09:34,626 --> 00:09:39,840
Aridification means an area slowly becoming dry and desert-like.

176
00:09:39,840 --> 00:09:43,850
When the monsoon weakened, the rain-fed rivers kept getting thinner.

177
00:09:43,850 --> 00:09:47,861
Was this the real reason this river dried up?

178
00:09:47,861 --> 00:09:51,470
Or did tectonics and climate together cause it?

179
00:09:51,470 --> 00:09:52,993
Now the most practical part.

180
00:09:52,993 --> 00:09:57,182
If you want to see this story yourself, where would you go?

181
00:09:57,182 --> 00:10:01,751
The good news is that its traces are still on the land today.

182
00:10:01,751 --> 00:10:04,797
You just have to keep your eyes open a little carefully. Come,

183
00:10:04,797 --> 00:10:06,320
let's walk onto the map.

184
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:08,561
Let's walk along the Sarsuti stream in Haryana.

185
00:10:08,561 --> 00:10:11,764
After the monsoon you can see water in it, and in summer, sand.

186
00:10:11,764 --> 00:10:14,966
Village people still link it with the Saraswati today.

187
00:10:14,966 --> 00:10:16,246
This is the very stream

188
00:10:16,246 --> 00:10:19,769
that an ISRO study connected with the old buried network.

189
00:10:19,769 --> 00:10:21,690
A small stream, but a big story.

190
00:10:21,690 --> 00:10:25,164
Then toward Rajasthan, look at the wide, dry bed of the Ghaggar.

191
00:10:25,164 --> 00:10:28,985
Paat means the bed of the river, where water once flowed.

192
00:10:28,985 --> 00:10:31,070
In many places it is so wide

193
00:10:31,070 --> 00:10:33,154
that it doesn't look like a small river.

194
00:10:33,154 --> 00:10:35,933
Nearby you find mounds of Harappan settlements,

195
00:10:35,933 --> 00:10:39,060
that is, high heaps of earth where digging has been done.

196
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:42,344
And now a place of faith, the Triveni Sangam in Prayagraj.

197
00:10:42,344 --> 00:10:46,358
Here the Ganga and Yamuna meet, you can see it with your own eyes.

198
00:10:46,358 --> 00:10:49,642
Folk tradition says the Saraswati also meets here,

199
00:10:49,642 --> 00:10:53,291
but underground, in an unseen form. This is a legend,

200
00:10:53,291 --> 00:10:55,116
it has no written source,

201
00:10:55,116 --> 00:10:57,670
But many people hold deep faith in it.

202
00:10:57,670 --> 00:11:02,207
Please note, this folk tale is linked to a story from the Mahabharata.

203
00:11:02,207 --> 00:11:06,332
The scriptures say the Saraswati went underground at Vinasana.

204
00:11:06,332 --> 00:11:10,870
Folk tradition says she flowed on underground and reached Prayagraj.

205
00:11:10,870 --> 00:11:14,995
We don't have any scientific proof of this yet.

206
00:11:14,995 --> 00:11:19,120
But standing at the Sangam, people feel her presence.

207
00:11:19,120 --> 00:11:21,532
So keep the whole story together.

208
00:11:21,532 --> 00:11:24,345
The scriptures speak of a mighty river.

209
00:11:24,345 --> 00:11:28,767
Satellites and drilling show a buried old river. Research says,

210
00:11:28,767 --> 00:11:31,983
the big Sutlej had moved away before the cities.

211
00:11:31,983 --> 00:11:35,198
And scholars differ on the name. There is evidence,

212
00:11:35,198 --> 00:11:37,610
but the final answer is still pending.

213
00:11:37,610 --> 00:11:41,484
Next time we will head to the sea, to the coast of Gujarat.

214
00:11:41,484 --> 00:11:45,359
Under the water there, another story lies buried, that of Dwarka.

215
00:11:45,359 --> 00:11:47,824
What the scriptures say, what the divers found,

216
00:11:47,824 --> 00:11:51,347
and what experts believe. Everything, with evidence,

217
00:11:51,347 --> 00:11:53,460
and with a label on every claim.

218
00:11:53,460 --> 00:11:54,740
Now a question for you.

219
00:11:54,740 --> 00:11:59,005
In your view, where did the Saraswati's water come from,

220
00:11:59,005 --> 00:12:02,843
from the Himalayan glaciers or from monsoon rain?

221
00:12:02,843 --> 00:12:05,829
Just write in the comments, glacier or rain.

222
00:12:05,829 --> 00:12:10,094
And if there is a dry river near your village,

223
00:12:10,094 --> 00:12:11,800
tell us its name too.
